+
+1. Add failing observable tests using custom AST rules: every listed extension, `.C` vs `.c`, actual PHP tag/mixed HTML/untagged INC, Blade partial state, modern C#, `.h` common/conflicting candidates, original Unicode/encoded spans, duplicate same-line observations, malformed syntax, zero/below/at/above budgets and modified custom semantics. Keep the prior exposed routing draft historical.
+2. Adopt the four exact tested grammar dependencies, preserving existing toolchain and parser runtime. Reconcile actual root lockfile/build dependencies; add language-specific semantic-node selection without enlarging existing seven-language candidate behavior.
+3. Implement ambiguous headers through two bounded candidate parses sharing the current cap allocation. Consolidate identical facts by original occurrence and identity; conflicting interpretation remains partial/unknown. Never infer configured build language, combine conflicting receiver bindings or give each candidate a full independent cap. Preserve custom AST matching and old rule IDs.
+4. Extend finite source-only normalized references through current SourceMatch and ObservationDraft mechanisms. Keep old recognized lexical vocabulary unchanged. Validate finite canonical names/exact parameter sets, reject unknown/contradictory/secret-bearing values and keep ast_pattern/syntactic/referenced assurance. New source handlers must not tokenize arbitrary cryptographic arguments into algorithm assets. No new required public fields, storage shape/version or evaluator policy branch.
+5. Update paired coverage docs to describe parser/routing foundation and its limits, without claiming future PHP/C#/C/C++ API packs complete. Record targeted results, inspect combined diff, obtain the existing implementation review identity, run required workspace/fmt/clippy/build and applicable feature checks, then obtain the existing test reviewer identity. Fix findings and retain final-source evidence before closing 077.
+
+The main agent owns all implementation and checks. Reviewers `/root/implementation_review` and `/root/test_review` are the only two identities for 076-083 and are reused. Unavoidable published-contract/high-risk changes trigger the existing human gate; routine compatible additions remain authorized. No push/release is selected.
